assets/music/ — provenance and licence notes
=============================================

Both files in this directory are excerpts of 1924 sound recordings by Bennie
Moten's Kansas City Orchestra that are in the PUBLIC DOMAIN IN THE UNITED STATES.
This file records where each excerpt came from, why it is public domain, what
was done to it and what could not be verified. It is a provenance record, not a
licence grant: nobody in this project owns these recordings, so nobody here can
license them; the files may be used because the US copyrights have expired.

Public-domain reasoning (United States)
---------------------------------------

Two layers of rights exist in a record like this, and both have expired in the
US:

1. The SOUND RECORDING. Pre-1972 US sound recordings are governed by
   17 U.S.C. § 1401 (the Classics Protection and Access Act, Title II of the
   Music Modernization Act, 2018). Under § 1401(a)(2), a recording first
   published between 1923 and 1946 is protected for 100 years after
   publication, with the term running to the end of the calendar year. A
   recording published in 1924 therefore entered the public domain on
   1 January 2025; one published in 1925 on 1 January 2026. Both sides below
   were recorded on 29 November 1924 and issued by OKeh in its 8000 "race"
   series no later than 1925 (OKeh 8184 and 8194 sit below OKeh 8242, which
   carries a May 1925 recording, and far below OKeh 8261, the first Louis
   Armstrong Hot Five disc, from a November 1925 session). Whether the discs
   shipped in late 1924 or early 1925 does not change the answer: either way
   the term ended by 31 December 2025. Recordings first published in 1926 or
   later are NOT yet public domain (1926 publications become free on
   1 January 2027), which is why the orchestra's later Victor sides were
   excluded.

2. The MUSICAL COMPOSITION. "South" and "Goofy Dust" were composed by Bennie
   Moten and Thamon Hayes and published in 1924. A work published in the US in
   1924 could receive at most 95 years of protection (Copyright Act of 1976 as
   extended in 1998), i.e. through 31 December 2019. The compositions have been
   public domain in the US since 1 January 2020.

This is a statement about US law only. Elsewhere the composition may still be
protected: in life-plus-70-years countries (the EU, the UK, Canada since 2023
and others) the co-composer Thamon Hayes (1898-1980) keeps "South" and "Goofy
Dust" in copyright until the end of 2050, even though the 1924 recordings
themselves are long out of term there. The project's reels are made and
published from Kansas City, Missouri; if a reel is ever released through a
publisher or platform that applies non-US rules, revisit this.

The archive.org uploader additionally marked both source items CC0 1.0
(https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/), which waives whatever
rights, if any, exist in the transfer/digitisation itself. Nothing here is
attribution-licensed (CC-BY) material.

