Processing applied (identical for both)
---------------------------------------

FFmpeg 4.4.2 (Ubuntu 22.04 build, aarch64), two passes, argument lists only:

  pass 1 (measure)
    ffmpeg -nostdin -y -ss START -t LENGTH -i SOURCE.mp3 -vn
      -af "highpass=f=60:poles=2,lowpass=f=8000:poles=2,
           loudnorm=I=-16:TP=-1.5:LRA=11:print_format=json"
      -f null -
  pass 2 (render, feeding back the measured values so loudnorm runs linear)
    ffmpeg -nostdin -y -ss START -t LENGTH -i SOURCE.mp3 -vn
      -af "highpass=f=60:poles=2,lowpass=f=8000:poles=2,
           loudnorm=I=-16:TP=-1.5:LRA=11:measured_I=..:measured_TP=..
                    :measured_LRA=..:measured_thresh=..:offset=..:linear=true,
           afade=t=in:st=0:d=1,afade=t=out:st=LENGTH-1:d=1,
           aformat=sample_rates=44100:channel_layouts=stereo"
      -c:a libmp3lame -b:a 128k -ar 44100 -ac 2 -map_metadata -1
      -id3v2_version 3 -metadata title=... -metadata comment=... OUT.mp3

  START/LENGTH: bed.mp3 6.230/89.430; bed-alt.mp3 36.780/71.060.

Why: the 60 Hz high-pass removes rumble and turntable thump that has no
musical content on an acoustic recording; the 8 kHz low-pass tames the
78-rpm hiss and crackle without touching the brass (these acoustic-era
transfers carry almost nothing above 4 kHz anyway); loudnorm puts both beds at
the same level so gain_db in a plan means the same thing for either; the 1 s
fades let the renderer loop or hard-cut the file without a click. No
de-clicking, noise reduction or EQ beyond the two filters was applied, so the
period surface noise is still audible at low level.

What was verified, and how
--------------------------

- Recording dates, matrix numbers and issue numbers were cross-checked against
  two independent discographies and agree with the uploader's item notes:
  * Discography of American Historical Recordings (UC Santa Barbara), which
    reproduces Laird & Rust, "Discography of OKeh Records, 1918-1934" (2004):
      https://adp.library.ucsb.edu/index.php/matrix/detail/2000200908/8769-South
      https://adp.library.ucsb.edu/index.php/matrix/detail/2000200911/8772-Goofy_dust
    (South: 11/29/1924, St. Louis, take B, OKeh 8194; Goofy dust: November
    1924, St. Louis, take A, OKeh 8184.)
  * The Online 78 rpm Discographical Project, OKeh 8000 series listing:
      https://www.78discography.com/OK8000.html
    (8184A Tulsa Blues 08771=A, 8184B Goofy Dust 08772=A, 8194A Vine Street
    Blues 08770=A, 8194B South 08769=B, all 11/29/1924.)
- The archive.org item metadata (https://archive.org/metadata/<identifier>)
  was fetched on 26 September 2026; both items carry licenseurl
  https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/ and the discographic
  notes quoted above.

What could NOT be verified
--------------------------

- Exact release (publication) dates for OKeh 8184 and 8194. Neither
  discography lists them; the pre-1926 publication is inferred from OKeh's
  catalogue numbering as explained above. Because both sides were recorded in
  November 1924 and issued in the ordinary course, an issue date after 1925 is
  not plausible, and even a 1925 issue is already out of term.
- Which physical disc and transfer chain the uploader used (the item notes
  do not say); the JV-… identifiers are the uploader's own numbering.

Considered and excluded
-----------------------

- "Kater Street Rag" and "Sister Honky Tonk" (OKeh 8277, matrices 9125 and
  9120, recorded Kansas City, May 1925). Musically a close second, and the
  recordings are 1925, but OKeh 8277 is late enough in the series that it may
  not have shipped until early 1926, which would keep the recording in term
  until 1 January 2027. Not worth the doubt when the 1924 sides are on theme.
  Note that the archive.org "airellebigbandarchive" copy of "Sister Honky
  Tonk" is labelled 1924; the discographies say May 1925.
- "18th Street Strut" (OKeh 8242, May 1925): almost certainly issued in 1925
  and a great Kansas City title, but a more uneven performance (solos with big
  dynamic swings) than either 1924 side; a possible future swap.
- "Thick Lip Stomp": the archive.org copy labelled 1923 is the Victor 20406
  recording of 13 December 1926 (Chicago), not eligible until 2027. The
  George Blood / Great 78 Project transfers of the 1927 Victor sides are
  likewise excluded.
